      "The National Provider Identifier, or NPI, is a unique 10-digit number assigned to health care providers in the United States. It was created under HIPAA's Administrative Simplification provisions to replace the many different identifiers payers used to assign. Today, the NPI is required on standard electronic transactions such as claims, eligibility inquiries, and remittances.",
      "## Who needs an NPI",
      "Any health care provider who conducts standard electronic transactions covered by HIPAA must have an NPI. In practice, that includes nearly every clinician and organization that bills insurance. Physicians, nurse practitioners, physician assistants, therapists, psychologists, dentists, chiropractors, pharmacies, hospitals, labs, and home health agencies all commonly have NPIs. Providers who do not bill electronically can still obtain one, and many payers require it regardless. Clinicians who order or refer services may also need an NPI so their information appears correctly on claims submitted by others.",
      "## Type 1 versus Type 2",
      "- Type 1 NPIs are for individual providers. A clinician gets one Type 1 NPI for life, and it stays the same even if they change jobs, states, or specialties.",
